WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.950
With the Dark Knight taking center stage in
The Lego Batman Movie in 2017, now's the perfect

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:11.500
time to look back at Phil Lord and Chris Miller's
kid-friendly masterpiece that started it all.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.630
2014’s The Lego Movie starred a boatload
of random characters from pretty much every

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.840
major pop culture property, but it almost
featured a few more.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.410
Oh, and did you know it almost didn't happen
at all?

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:24.080
Here's the untold truth of The Lego Movie.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.320
Tough sell

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.480
Producer Dan Lin came up with the idea for
a shared universe Lego film, and travelled

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.260
all the way to the toy company’s headquarters
in Denmark to pitch the idea in 2008.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.480
But, the Lego execs weren’t all that excited
to license their brand.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.550
Long before the film, Lego was already one
of the biggest players in the toy industry,

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:48.040
and executives were worried about potentially
risking that position with a lackluster movie.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.399
Lucky for everyone, Lin went ahead and had
an incredibly detailed treatment put together

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:54.809
— and the Lego folks were sold.

00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:57.070
It almost starred R2-D2

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.530
From Batman to Lando Calrissian, The Lego
Movie jammed in fictional characters by the

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:02.530
truckload.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.930
But it almost featured a much bigger Star
Wars connection.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:09.670
Lord and Miller originally planned to have
R2-D2 be a main character in the film, joining

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.650
up with Emmet's group for pretty much the
entire adventure.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.840
Miller described his role as on par with the
one that eventually went to Batman.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.700
But Lucasfilm was reluctant to offer up a
Star Wars character for such a major role

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.700
in the story.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.100
The fact that all these deals were coming
together against the clock of Lucasfilm's

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.460
2012 sale to Disney probably didn't make it
easier.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.600
Fortunately, the movie losing R2 meant it
gained Batman — and everything's better

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.600
with Batman.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.869
"Batman we love you!"

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:38.209
"Thank you, I'm blushing super-hard under
the mask."

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.150
"Thanks Batman!"

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:43.670
First Flash

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.119
The Flash has been on the big screen a few
times now thanks to Batman v Superman: Dawn

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:50.299
of Justice and Suicide Squad.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:54.570
But The Lego Movie actually beat both of those
movies to the punch with its own take on the

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:55.780
Scarlet Speedster.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.310
Sure, he's only on screen for a little less
than five seconds, but considering how much

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:05.630
Warner Bros. and DC are banking on the big-screen
Justice League universe, it's kind of funny

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:10.309
to think something as wacky as The Lego Movie
actually got there first.

00:02:10.309 --> 00:02:11.450
Deep Cuts

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:15.510
Though the film packed in a bunch of Easter
eggs about pop culture in general, it also

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.779
included some subtle deep cuts for hardcore
Lego nerds.

00:02:18.779 --> 00:02:19.779
One of the best?

00:02:19.779 --> 00:02:22.559
The little break at the center of Benny the
spaceman's helmet.

00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:26.969
Back in the day, that was a common defect
in those types of Lego mini-fig helmets, so

00:02:26.969 --> 00:02:30.989
the filmmakers included it as a nod for longtime
Lego fans.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:31.989
Lego Kiss

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:36.309
Sure, The Lego Movie was wild and weird, but
there was apparently one line they couldn't

00:02:36.309 --> 00:02:38.620
cross: A mini-fig make-out session.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.949
Kind of like this, probably:

00:02:40.949 --> 00:02:42.949
(hardcore smooching)

00:02:42.949 --> 00:02:46.959
"Ugh, that's very disturbing."

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:51.169
In the original cut, the Scuba Cops blew off
their patrols to go make out with some mermaids,

00:02:51.169 --> 00:02:56.309
but it apparently made parents uncomfortable
during test screenings, so they cut the scene.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:03:00.459
Wyldstyle also laid a smooch on Batman at
one point, but that too was axed.

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:02.249
The cameos

00:03:02.249 --> 00:03:06.049
The Lego Movie had almost as many cameos as
it does lego bricks.

00:03:06.049 --> 00:03:11.139
The Star Wars franchise was well-represented
with some great cameos, and a few even reprised

00:03:11.139 --> 00:03:13.359
their roles from the original trilogy.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:18.909
Billy Dee Williams voiced Lando Calrissian,
while Anthony Daniels once again voiced C-3PO.

00:03:18.909 --> 00:03:23.879
Lord and Miller also included some nods to
their previous flicks, most notably 21 Jump

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:26.299
Street stars Channing Tatum and Jonah Hill.

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:29.639
Tatum voiced Superman and Hill brought Green
Lantern to life.

00:03:29.639 --> 00:03:33.889
If Henry Cavill doesn't pan out as Superguy,
at least Tatum has some experience as the

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:34.889
Man of Steel.

00:03:34.889 --> 00:03:38.489
And hey, they're making a new Green Lantern
movie, right?

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:39.669
Real Business

00:03:39.669 --> 00:03:44.330
The film's big bad Lord Business runs the
company Octan in the film, which at first

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:46.290
seems to be just another made-up company.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:50.979
As it turns out, it is a fictional company
— but a real fake one actually created by

00:03:50.979 --> 00:03:52.779
Lego back in 1992.

00:03:52.779 --> 00:03:57.959
The company was a fictional brand of gasoline
that was used in some early 1990s play sets,

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:01.340
and the creative team resurrected it for throwback
kicks.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.669
Slow Motion

00:04:02.669 --> 00:04:07.379
The film has one of the most original looks
in modern CGI, with visual effects designed

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:09.439
to emulate the look of stop-motion.

00:04:09.439 --> 00:04:12.850
From the built-in scuffs, to the click and
clack when the blocks come together, they

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.239
work really hard to make this movie look tactile
and real.

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:19.940
But had they actually gone the stop-motion
route, it would've reportedly taken about

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:24.740
a decade to make — and it would have required
a mind-boggling 15 million Lego bricks.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:28.340
At that point, you could literally build your
own full-fledged Lego Land.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.030
The cool factor

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:34.860
When all was said and done, there's no denying
that Will Arnett's Lego Batman was the breakout

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.939
character from The Lego Movie, and the fact
that he got his own movie in 2017 is proof.

00:04:39.939 --> 00:04:43.800
But it turns out the producers only used him
because they needed someone with infinite

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:44.800
cool factor.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.939
The creative team originally wanted to use
someone else for WyldStyle’s boyfriend,

00:04:48.939 --> 00:04:52.830
but went with Batman to ensure he was extra
awesome to intimidate Emmet.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.430
Because, seriously, no one's cooler than Batman.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.620
(beatboxing)

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.160
"Oh, hey I'm sorry.

00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:06.319
Just laying down some dope tracks here and
didn't see ya there."

00:05:06.319 --> 00:05:07.319
Thanks for watching!

00:05:07.319 --> 00:05:09.650
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.910
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

